WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.014
Least privilege applies to every file
operation.

2
00:00:03.094 --> 00:00:06.926
A function that only needs to read a
configuration file should not open it for

3
00:00:07.006 --> 00:00:07.784
modification.

4
00:00:07.864 --> 00:00:12.055
A process that only needs to write reports
should not have broad access to unrelated

5
00:00:12.135 --> 00:00:13.152
application state.

6
00:00:13.232 --> 00:00:17.543
Narrow access reduces accidental damage
and limits what can happen when another part

7
00:00:17.623 --> 00:00:19.539
of the program behaves unexpectedly.

8
00:00:19.619 --> 00:00:22.672
Read-only access is safer when no
modification is required.

9
00:00:22.752 --> 00:00:26.763
It communicates intent to reviewers,
reduces the chance of accidental writes,

10
00:00:26.843 --> 00:00:30.075
and can allow the operating system to
enforce part of the design.

11
00:00:30.155 --> 00:00:33.628
Similarly, append behavior, overwrite
behavior, create behavior,

12
00:00:33.708 --> 00:00:37.360
and truncate behavior should be chosen
because they match the workflow,

13
00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:40.612
not because a convenient mode happened to
work during testing.

14
00:00:40.692 --> 00:00:43.087
Execute permission deserves special
caution.

15
00:00:43.167 --> 00:00:45.502
Ordinary data files, logs, reports,
uploads,

16
00:00:45.582 --> 00:00:48.755
and caches should not receive execute
permission as a default.

17
00:00:48.835 --> 00:00:50.631
When files are meant to be executed,

18
00:00:50.711 --> 00:00:54.303
that is a distinct workflow with its own
trust and review requirements.

19
00:00:54.383 --> 00:00:57.316
Most application-created data should not
be executable.

20
00:00:57.396 --> 00:01:00.150
Least privilege also applies to the
process identity.

21
00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:04.361
Services should run with file system
privileges limited to the storage areas they

22
00:01:04.441 --> 00:01:05.219
actually need.

23
00:01:05.299 --> 00:01:08.412
If the service only needs a work directory
and a log directory,

24
00:01:08.492 --> 00:01:11.186
it should not be able to write broadly
across the host.

25
00:01:11.266 --> 00:01:13.361
Directory design, account permissions,

26
00:01:13.441 --> 00:01:16.016
and application code should reinforce each
other.

27
00:01:16.096 --> 00:01:20.167
The practical value of least privilege is
that it reduces the impact of mistakes.

28
00:01:20.247 --> 00:01:23.539
If validation, parsing, path handling, or
business logic fails,

29
00:01:23.619 --> 00:01:27.451
restrictive file access can keep the
failure from reaching unrelated data.

30
00:01:27.531 --> 00:01:30.165
Permissions are not a replacement for
correct code,

31
00:01:30.245 --> 00:01:32.340
but they are an important layer around it.
